## KEYWORDS
porous three-dimensional (3D) carbon-based materials, hydrogen production, advanced synthesis
## Introduction
The impending global energy crisis has prompted intense research to focus on energy conversion and  storage systems [l]. Electrocatalytic oxygen reduction reaction (ORR), hydrogen evolution reaction (HER), as well as oxygen evolution reaction (OER) play indispensable roles in multifarious energy technologies such as fuel cells and water splitting [2, 3]. However, the high cost and severe scarcity of precious metals limit their applications. To enhance HER catalytic activities, and maximize hydrophilicities and active site exposure, many works, focusing on the adjustments of structural defects, establishing homophase/heterojunction, growth of nanowires and nanoparticles, as well as nanosheets on conductive substrates have been done in recent years [4-9].
In general, to make great improvements in easy recombination of photogenerated electron-hole pairs and insufficient active sites on a substrate, precious metal needs be used as little as possible in developing new two-dimensional (2D) photocatalysts [10]. Direct use of “cheap metal" with huge resource reserves and low prices also provides a way to lower the budget [1l]. Those modifications of traditional catalysts can effectively raise the efficiency of quantum yield, making industrial applications possible. Although plenty of breakthroughs have been achieved in the past few years, "new generation mesoporous materials", named for nanoporous carbon-based materials, have drawn the attention of many researchers, recently. This is because porous carbon-based materials normally have clear advantages of lower cost for precursor preparation, and more facile synthesized method [12]. More importantly, excellent physical and chemical properties endow those advanced porous carbon-based materials with outstanding biocompatibility and chemical stability, especially under non-oxidative conditions. The three-dimensional (3D) porous carbon-based material refers to making holes in the interior of 2D carbon-based materials or changing the morphology to make spheres, scaffolds (sponge and foam), gels, etc.,with different sizes of pores distributed inside and on the surface. The 3D porous carbon-based structure is characterized by highly developed specific surface area and abundant porous structure that is connected or closed with each other. These catalysts with 3D porous structures, during the catalytic reaction, can speed up the transmission rate of ions and gases, and the larger specific surface area also provides the possibility for more active sites. Compared to regular materials, porous carbon-based also has a low density, high thermal conductivity, good electrical conductivity, and mechanical stability [13-20].
As shown in Fig. 1, although the concept of structuring porous templating carbon-based was demonstrated [2l], some emerging applications (i.e. gas adsorption, catalysis, storage of $\mathrm{H}_{2},$ Li, and other metals) that require high specific areas and pore volumes have been more widely applied since the discovery of porous single-walled carbon nanotubes (SWNTs) [22]. Via a simple thermal chemical vapor deposition (CVD) method, Tao et al. [24] prepared porous branched carbon nanofibers (CNFs) having irregular pores of diameter ranged from 8 to $20~\mathrm{nm}$ .However, porous carbon-based materials were also used as a coating or adsorbent in biomedicine to replace polymers that were thought to initiate and sustain inflammation and contribute to late complications [25]. Recently, carbon-based materials, such as graphene, carbon nanotubes (CNTs), and biomass-derived porous carbon-based materials, have been investigated extensively as ORR and OER electrocatalysts. However, to further control the pore structure, obtaining highly porous, oriented, and interlinked carbon-based nanosheets or novel bi-functional electrocatalyst for ORR and OER remains challenging [26-28]. $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ is considered as the most promising photocatalyst due to its unique electronic structure, outstanding stability, non-toxicity, and low cost. However, its limited surface area, poor light-harvesting eficiency, and rapid recombination of light-induced charge carriers limit in HER. To overcome the above shortcomings, porous_or hierarchical multi-component photocatalysts were constructed by coupling with other visible-light semiconductors to improve the efficiency of HER [29]. Another strategy is to take heteroatom doping-containing metal-organic frameworks (MOFs)-derived carbon-based materials and/or covalent organic frameworks (COFs) asprecursors,whichdemonstrateexcellent electrochemical performance [30, 31]. Being a new type of carbonbased material, MXenes are also attracting more and more attention. Layered MXenes gels were obtained through selfassembly and other methods, which not only greatly raise the specific surface area of the resultant materials but also more importantly, improve their optoelectronic performance [32,33]. However, some toxic strong acid reagents are inevitably used in the process of preparing 3D porous MXenes. Recently, biomassderived porous carbon-based materials have emerged, which are expected to be the precursors of new porous carbon materials due to their abundant resources, low density, high environmental protection, and adjustable pore size [34, 35].
This review is intended to provide an overview of recent research progresses in the synthesis of advanced and novel porous carbon-based materials for highly efficient photo/electrocatalyst HER, hydrogen release from methanolysis of sodium borohydride, and HER by methane decomposition and pyrolysis-gasification. As presented in Fig.2, we concluded in the first section five kinds of advanced and novel porous carbon-based materials including porous CNTs, porous graphene, biomass-derived porous carbonbased materials, COFs and/or MOFs, and porous MXenes. The advantages and characteristics of these catalytic materials for HER would also be described. In the second section,we summarized four main mechanisms for the HER through porous carbon-based materials, followed by discussing diversified and novel synthetic methods for further improving the activity of HER in the third section. Eventually, the properties of different pore structures and the roles they played in catalytic processes will be classified in the last section. Regarding HER, special attention will be paid to developing porous carbon-based nanocomposite materials for electrochemical and photochemical activity.
## 2 Porous carbon-based materials
Porous carbon-based materials not only have the advantages of carbon-based materials, but also improve the shortcomings of carbon-based materials. Thus, the materials have high specific surface area, and adjustable physical and chemical properties, moreover, the preparation process is environmentally friendly and convenient, showing excellent application prospects in extensive fields.
## 2.1 Porous CNTs
CNTs could be considered as an inner hollow columnar structure rolled of a nanometer graphite plate having a large length/diameter ratio without any aperture on the tube wall first prepared by Iijima in 1991 [36]. In an electrolyzing HER process, catalysts are less stable in acidic electrolytes, and a large amount of heat is often generated. Therefore, electrodes are required to have both good electrical conductivity and stable physical properties. Carbon materials are perfect candidates; to some extent, the structures of CNTs are similar to those of graphene sheets, which have good electrical conductivity. Especially, superconductivity could be formed when the diameter of a tube is lowered to $1\mathrm{nm}$ Beyond that, the heat transfer performance of CNTs is still excellent. The aspect ratio of CNTs is quite large, which can effectively enhance the thermal conductivity of composite materials by diffusing heat along its axial direction. However, it needs to face the problems of low specific surface area and disordered pore structure. Nevertheless, these problems can be overcome by those porous carbon-based materials endowed larger surface areas, low relative density, and high specific strength.
Wang et al. [37] successfully prepared sponge-like nickel phosphide-CNTs $(\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{P}/\mathrm{CNTs})$ hybrid electrodes. This unique 3D sponge-structured carbon-based scaffold has more distinctive advantageous features than 2D carbon-based materials (Figs. 3(a)-3(c)). Specifically, 3D materials are more readily applicable for electrolytes having a wider $\mathrm{\pH}$ range. They also possess superior chemical stability and compressibility, as well as higher porosity. Moreover, these hybrid electrodes had achieved one of the best values reported for earth-abundant HER at that time. As shown in statistics of the cathodic current density, the overpotential needs to reach about $226~\mathrm{\mV}$ to obtain 100 $\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}.$ On the other hand, Ni has directly grown amorphously on the substrate, avoiding the use of conductive additives and polymer binders, so as to avoid the active sites being covered, resulting in a loss of conductivity and catalytic HER activity. Gao et al. [38] adopted a two-step chemical vapor deposition method to fabricate heteroatom-doped 3D graphene-CNTs (B-N-G
CNTs), which showed great cycling durability in acidic solution, especially when it is used as electrodes, high current density carrying capacity and fast electron transfer could be found (Figs. 3(d)-3(f). Currently, atom-doped carbon-based materials have been considered as one of the most suitable potential catalysts for ORR or HER. Via density functional theory (DFT), these novel catalysts for HER and ORR reference barrier were examined. It was found that the reference potential barriers of HER and ORR can both decrease simultaneously and the depressed barrier altitude of the reference potential barriers are 1.09948, 1.79397, and $2.40384\:\mathrm{eV};$ 、respectively. Kang et al.[39] used a macroporous CNTs skeleton to composite with $\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}$ to fabricate microspheres. The CNTs skeleton not only greatly improved the electrical conductivity but also controlled the growth of $\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}$ nanocrystals during the synthesis process, which improves the activity of $\mathrm{CoSe}_{2},$ and finally raises the electrocatalytic activity. The $\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}$ -CNTs composite microspheres have an extremely large number of macroporous structure, which makes the penetration of acidic electrolyte to the center of each microsphere easier. Therefore, the porosity of the composite microspheres (Figs. 3(g) and $3(\mathrm{h})\dot{}$ leads to different HER electrocatalytic performances. The Tafel slope of the $\mathrm{CoSe}_{2}$ -CNTs composite was 37.8 mV-dec-, which was comparable to the commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ power, $30.2\mathrm{mV\cdotdec^{-1}}$
## 2.2 Porous ${\bf g}{\bf-C}_{3}{\bf N}_{4}$
Due to its unique electronic structure, tunable energy band structure, and excellent visible light responsiveness, $\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4}}$ is called “star materials” [40]. Being an organic semiconductor polymer containing only carbon and nitrogen elements, $\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4}}$ has been widely applied in photocatalysis, especially in the fields of HER and pollutant degradation to  solve energy and environmental pollution problems [4l,42]. Unfortunately, the current synthesized method is limited to thermal polymerization [43-45]. This approach has the disadvantages that the obtained materials are mostly bulk and have a small specific surface area [46],which leads difficulty in transferring photogenerated carriers [47], and rapid recombination of photogenerated electron-hole pairs [48]. There are two strategies to improve the photocatalytic activity  of $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ ： lowering the material to nanometer size, and creating porous structure to raise the reaction sites and improve its light absorption capacity and multiple scattering effects [49, 50].
Huang et al. [5l] developed a facile urea and KOH-assisted thermal polymerization strategy to prepare defect-modified thinlayer porous $\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4},}$ named KCN (Figs. 4(a) and 4(b)), combining with cyano groups, nitrogen vacancies, and mesopores. This strategy stimulatingly controlled the thickness, pore structure, and dopant atomic defects; significantly raised the specific area, improved the light-trapping ability, and enhanced  the photoexcited charge carrier separation efficiency. The obtained thin-layered and mesoporous KCN (mKCN) was favorable for the excitation of electrons from valence band (VB) to the empty orbital of conduction band (CB) and forms more unpaired orbital electrons which can respond in a wide spectral range. Thus, the HER of mKCN can reach $1.557\mathrm{\mmol{\cdot}h^{-1}{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ under visible light (Fig.4(c)), about 48.5 times higher than pristine $\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4}}$ with an average apparent quantum yield (AQY) up to $18.5\%$ and up to $0.82\%$ at $500\mathrm{nm}$
Through doping non-metalic heteroatoms, Zhou et al. [52] synthetized porous S-doped $\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4}}$ nanosheets (PCNS). With Sdoped $\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4}}$ (CNS) as the precursor, $\mathrm{\DeltaNH_{4}C l}$ as foaming agent can be decomposed into bubbles ( $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ and HCl gas) during the polymerization process, without using $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{HF}_{2}$ or strong acid to remove residues, and any carbon remaining can also be avoided. The as-obtained PCNS is tunable and adjustable because the gaseous $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ and HCl released act as bubble templates promoting the formation of a uniformly dispersed perforated, layered structure, inhibiting the agglomeration caused by the heterogeneous heat transfer (Figs. 4(d) and 4(e)). The improvements in specific surface area and visible light absorption make the photocatalytic HER activity of PCNS 25.3-fold and 3.8- fold larger than that of bulk $\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4}}$ (BCN) and CNS, respectively (Fig.4(f).
Ai et al. [53] used a simple and efficient salt-template-assisted method to grow $\mathrm{MnO}_{x}$ on porous $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ This NaCl template approach for constructing 3D porous high-surface-area structure can also induce the coordination of Na and N atoms, furthermore the formation of an internal donor-acceptor system with accompanying cyano groups for efficient charge transfer and separation (Figs. $4(\mathrm{g)}$ and $4\mathrm{(h)}\dot{}$ ). The bandgap of $\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4}}$ increased with the growth of $\mathrm{MnO}_{x},$ and the CB level is negatively shifted for enhanced reduction. The resulting porous $\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4}}$ was approximately 18-fold more active than pristine $\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4}}$
## 2.3 COFs and MOFs-derived carbon-based materials
COFs are organic porous materials with an ordered crystal structure, where the connection nodes are organic, not metallic.
The advantages of COFs include adjustable pore size, uniform pore distribution, and large specific surface area. It is a new class of heterogeneous catalysts, known as “organic zeolite" [54]. The type of catalysts can be divided into intrinsic and supported type. The former is based on a “bottom-up” strategy to embed catalytic active centers into COFs framework [55]. The latter loads catalysts, using COFs as a carrier, and builds heterogeneous by loading metal particles or ions on the substrate to overcome shortcomings of traditional homogeneous catalysis, such as, diffculty of separation of reaction products and poor catalyst performance [56]. MOFs are porous coordination polymers formed by coordinating metal ions and organic ligands. Metal atoms or clusters are used as organic connection nodes, moreover, in the case of COFs, the structure and pores of MOFs are also adjustable and tunable [57].Both COFs and MOFs can be used for catalytic applications, also the catalysts can be prepared into 2D and 3D structures. As the pore size of MOFs is related to the selected organic ligands, MOFs with relatively small pore size and high porosity are generally applied in gas adsorption and separation [58]. On the other hand, MOFs with large pore sizes are selected in catalytic applications [59]. While COFs are mainly used in gas storage and luminescence [60, 61].
Generally, most of COFs materials do not dissolve or decompose upon dissolution, which greatly limits their utility. Gao et al. [62] first prepared water-soluble 3D COFs (sCOF-101). This sCOF-10l is considered a new 3D ordered polymers material (Fig 5(a)),which is highly stable in harsh acidic and basic media. It does not suffer from interpenetration, which substantially lowers pore size. It is able to enrich $\mathrm{Ru}^{2+}$ complex photosensitizers and redox-active polyoxometalates, yielding a significant increase in their photocatalytic activity. The 3D COFs catalyst can consequently photocatalytic reduction of protons into hydrogen. Song et al. [63] anchored ultrafine metal nanoparticles on porous MOFs (Figs. 5(b) and 5(c)). They found that MOFs can not only effectively confine the aggregation of nanoparticles but also improve the interaction between nanoparticles and finally enhance catalysis activity. The surface of MOFs is complexed with metals and abundant groups, making it rich in Pd-Ag electrons. This is beneficial to the dissociation of the O-H bond of formic acid molecules (Fig.5(d)), thereby facilitating the formation of intermediates in the initial step of the dehydrogenation reaction. Li et al. [64] prepared hierarchically mesoporous titanium phosphonates-based MOFs through a coupled-template method (Fig. 5(e)). The well-structured spheres possessed a large specific surface area, ordered mesostructure, and large secondary nanopores, affording a smooth pathway for fast transportation of electrolytes and products. Under visible light $\mathit{\Omega}\lambda\ >\ 400\ \mathrm{\nm})$ irradiation, a remarkable rate for HER, $945\ \mathrm{mmol}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1};$ can be reached, and after $16\mathrm{~h~}$ of circulation (Figs. 5(f) and $5(\mathrm{g})$ )，the stability is still maintained.
## 2.4 Porous MXenes
Recently, MXenes have emerged as the largest advanced 2D materials. The chemical formulas of these transition metal carbide/nitride can be expressed generally as $\mathrm{M}_{n+1}\mathrm{X}_{n}\mathrm{T}_{x}$ where $n$ ranges from 1 to 4, M represents early transition metals (e.g., ${\mathrm{Ti}},$ Zr, V, and Mo). X is C or N, $\mathrm{T}_{x}$ stands for surface groups, usually including -OH, -O,-F, and $-S$ [65]. Therefore, the surface of MXenes is full of highly tunable metal components and abundant functional groups, rendering them with excellent metal conductivity, great stability, and outstanding softness [66-68]. With the introduction of porous structures, it has unique advantages in regulating electrical conductivity, permittivity, modulatingion/guest and even electromagneticwave transmission, and guiding the loading and distribution of other functional materials [69]. The unique 3D inter-linked porous MXenes framework has ultrathin hole walls, its electronic structure is optimized, and therefore, shows excellent performance in HER[70].
Through a top-down synthesis method, Shen et al. [71] synthesized $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\boldsymbol{x}}.$ -reduced graphene oxide (MX-RGO) networkconfined Ni-Fe layered double hydroxide (LDHs) nanosheets (Fig.6(a)). This carbon hybrid architecture exposes the complete catalytic active sites and optimizes the electronic structure, which not only maintains excellent electrochemical performance but also accelerates the transport of ions and electrons during catalysis. This unique Ni-Fe layered metallic double hydroxides nanosheet structure can not only effectively reduce the re-aggregation and/or packing of LDHs and $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\boldsymbol{x}}$ but also expose more LDHs edge sites and oxygen functional groups on $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\boldsymbol{x}}$ to greatly enhance the HER activity. As shown in Figs. 6(b) and $6(\mathrm{c})_{:}$ 。the incorporation of LDHs into the $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ graphene system can improve the overall water-splitting performance due to the dissociation of $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ molecule much easier on the LDHs edge sites. Although the C sites in graphene exhibit relatively low HER activity, the interconnected C sites in 3D LDHs/MX-RGO frame structure can not only facilitate the transport of electrolytes but also reduce the charge transfer resistance in the chemical system, which also contributes to improving HER efficiency.
Taking advantage of the unique property of the negatively charged surface of ${\mathrm{Ti}}_{3}{\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{T}}_{3}$ Lv et al. [72] wrapped MXenes sheet and the porous cobalt phosphide by two-step electrodeposition, and uniformly deposited them on the 3D porous framework successfully to prepare $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P}/\mathrm{N}@\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x}@\mathrm{NF}$ (Fig.6(d)). It exhibits excellent activity in HER reaction, with an overpotential of $29~\mathrm{mV}$ in $1\mathrm{~M~KOH}$ and a Tafel slope of $69~\mathrm{mV\cdotdec^{-1}}$ .The electron density difference was used to study the charge redistribution and clarify the 3D structure of improving the HER activity, indicating that electrons are concentrated at the interface of the catalyst synthesized by two-step electrodeposition heterojunction. Then due to the relatively strong electronegative N atoms being more active than $\mathrm{~P~}{}$ atoms the electrons are transferred from $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P/N}$ to $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\boldsymbol{x}}$ thus, proving the stabilizing effect of multi-heterostructure interfaces and 3D porous structure (Fig. 6(e)).
At the present stage, developing stable and corrosion-resistant catalysts which can be applied under various $\mathrm{pH}$ conditions for
HER is extremely important. Therefore, lowering the energy consumption and cost of water splitting becomes a hotspot. To further improve the HER activity, Kong et al. [73] prepared porous Ni foams with $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\boldsymbol{x}}$ nanosheets $(\mathrm{Ru}/c-\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x}/\mathrm{NF})$ that can remain stable in an alkaline electrolyte (Fig.6(f)). These unique MXenes nanosheets with oxygen-containing functional groups $(-\mathrm{O}$ and -OH groups) can provide metal anchoring sites, facilitating the construction of hydrophilic wrinkled surfaces and stabilizing catalytically active sites on porous Ni foams (Fig. $6(\mathrm{g)}$ ， thereby enhancing the HER activity. The presence of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\boldsymbol{x}}$ promotes a strong adsorption of $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ and accelerates H-OH splitting, thereby lowering the kinetic barrier of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ dissociation (Figs. 6(h) and $6(\mathrm{i})_{\cdot}^{\cdot}$ ）
## 2.5 Biomass-derived porous carbon-based materials
Exfoliating MXenes, calcining $\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4},}$ or pre-processing CNTs not only consumes a lot of work and time, but also leads to a low precursor yield, low purity, and use of additional additives,which limit the use of other carbon-based materials. In contrast, abundant biocarbon sources can easily be synthesized from carbon-rich biological wastes on earth, such as coffee beans, banana peels,waste okara, and soybean,most biomass-derived porous carbon-based materials come from food residues or domestic wastes [74-77]. Biomass-derived porous carbon-based materials can be obtained by  vacuum drying and hightemperature calcination in a mufle furnace. However, adjusting the pore size and distribution is not straightforward. Nevertheless, biomass-derived porous carbon-based materials have gained extensive attention due to their low cost, easy availability, and environmentally friendly.
Sathiskumar et al. [78] developed the solvent-free synthesis and biomass-derived N-doped porous carbon (N/PC) from low-cost golden shower pod biomass (GSB). The key factor is N doping in a porous carbon framework, which changes both the electronic and chemical structures, and improves the efficiency of electrochemical performance. From field emission scanning electron microscopy (FE-SEM) characterization, it was observed that N/PC has a relatively large and self-organized porous structure, showing a strong trifunctional catalytic performance, namely HER, OER, and ORR (Fig. 7(a)). Honey is one of the most beneficial foods in nature, rich in carbohydrates, water, and protein, and it has small amounts of amino acids that provide C and N atoms. Wang et al. [79] proposed using a laser beam as the replacement for high-temperature pyrolysis to induce localized carbonization on the substrate. The proposed method can save both time and cost, which attracted a lot of attention (Fig. 7(b)). As seen in Figs. 7(c) and 7(d), the porous structure becomes relatively rough after doping with Ru. As the number of doping increases, the nanosheets become thicker with a less pore structure. The 3D biomass-derived porous nanosheets are beneficial to facilitate electrolyte penetration and HER. Electrode assembly catalysts usually require the addition of polymer binders and conductiveadditives, which will inevitably lead to microstructure damage. To overcome this difficulty, Cao et al. [80] conversed bean sprouts (BS) into biomass-derived porous carbonbased materials through a high-temperature carbonization method without utilizing any physical and/or chemical activation techniques (Fig. 7(e)). High-temperature pyrolysis is a key step to obtaining good electrical conductivity, and it is beneficial to form a hierarchical porous structure and raise the specific surface area. The highly graphitized biomass-derived porous carbon-based film prepared by pomelo peel as the carbon substrate has a large specific surface area. As shown in Figs. 7(f) and $7(\mathrm{g)}$ the open and interconnected macropores yield new directional built-in electron transfer pathways, and provide also abundant reaction sites. The as-obtained biomass-derived porous carbon-based materials exhibit a high specific capacitance of $203.8~\mathrm{F{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ at $1\ \mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and a small Tafel slope of $98~\mathrm{mV\cdotdec^{-1}}$ and have a long-term immense stability.
## 3 Porous carbon-based materials for HER
At present, $90\%$ of industrial hydrogen is still produced by steam reforming of fosil fuels, which not only consumes a lot of energy but also releases a lot of greenhouse gases. This chapter summarizes four more environmentally friendly and efficient methods to produce hydrogen based on porous carbon-based materials:(i） photo/electro-catalytic HER;(ii） HER from methanolysis of sodium borohydride; (ii) HER by methane decomposition; (iv) HER from pyrolysis-gasification.
## 3.1 Mechanisms
## 3.1.1 Photo/electrocatalytic HER
As it is named, photocatalysis implies“photo $^+$ catalysis"，where energy comes from a light source (artificial or natural). When catalyst is irradiated, although itself does not change, it can promote chemical reactions, stimulate $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ to form free negative ions with great oxidative power, and decompose harmful organic substances and some inorganic substances. The principle of semiconductor photocatalysis can be briefly summarized as following. When the energy of incident light exceeds the bandgap energy of the semiconductor, the electrons in the VB of the semiconductor are excited to the CB.Highly active electrons with strong negative charges are generated on the CB, while positively charged holes are generated on the VB, thereby generating highly active electrons [81]. After these electron-hole pairs migrate to catalyst surface, they interact with dissolved $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and finally generate ·OH with a high chemical oxidation activity. After separation and migration, the photogenerated electrons and holes will be captured by the reaction sites on the photocatalyst surface and participate photocatalytic reaction [82]. However, non-toxic and stable porous carbon-based materials could be used as an ideal template for packaging nanosheets and suppressing the difficulty of charge transport caused by the aggregation and accumulation of nanoparticles. Porous carbon-based materials have excellent electrical conductivity, large specific surface area, and excellent performance in HER. For instance, using triethanolamine $(10\mathrm{vol}.\%)$ as a sacrificial electron donor and Pt as a cocatalyst, photogenerated electrons can be rapidly transferred to porouscarbon-based materials and separated from photogenerated holes. The electrons will then be injected and aggregated on the uniformly dispersed Pt nanoparticles, reducing water molecules (or $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ )to $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ 、As a conductive medium, porous carbon-based materials can effectively capture and transport electrons (Figs. 8(a) and $8(\mathrm{b})_{,}^{\cdot}$ . However, a further increase in carbon loading results in a slight decrease in inactivity,which is attributed to the“shielding effect” caused by overloading [83].
Furthermore, the catalysts lacking active sites will reduce their performance of HER. Therefore, tremendous attentions have been paid to exploring HER active sites of catalysts. Zhang et al. [84] compared porous $\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4}}$ and pristine $\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4}}$ catalysts under visible light irradiation, and it was found that the former showed better performance of HER and phase stability. These results suggested that structural defects in the active site can promote extended light absorption, hole transfer to accelerate its reactivity.
In addition to photocatalysis, electrocatalysis is also the next leading technology for sustainable energy conversion. 3D porous carbon-based materials have many distinct advantages in the field of electrocatalysis, including active sites at the edges that are more active than those on the surface or bulk. The unique 3D porous interconnected structures allow electrolyte diffusion and keep good stability and high performance in harsh environments (extreme $\mathrm{\pH}$ value). In reaction of water electrolysis, the HER reaction occurs at the cathode, which is a reaction in which $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ ions obtain two $\mathrm{e^{-}}$ to form $\mathrm{H}_{2},$ the mechanism can be expressed as (Eqs.(1)-(3)) [85]
$$
\mathrm{H^{+}+e^{-}+M=M H_{a d s}}
$$
$$
\mathrm{H}^{+}+\mathrm{MH}_{\mathrm{ads}}+\mathrm{e}^{-}=\mathrm{H}_{2}+\mathrm{M}
$$
$$
\mathbf{M}\mathbf{H}_{\mathrm{ads}}=2\mathbf{M}+\mathbf{H}
$$
The Volmer process (Eq. (1)), in which protons gain electrons to generate adsorbed hydrogen atoms on the catalyst surface, is an initial electron transfer process. If the Volmer reaction is the ratecontrolling step of the whole reaction, then the principle of the Volmer reaction process can be regarded as the adsorption principle. When the rate control step is regarded as Heyrovsky (Eq. (2)) (the $\mathrm{MH_{ads}}$ generated by the Volmer reaction is further combined with proton in the electrolyte, and at the same time obtains an electron to complete the HER reaction), the principle of this reaction process can be  regarded  as electrochemical desorption mechanism. If the rate-controlling step is regarded as the Tafel reaction (Eq. (3)), the principle of this reaction process can be regarded as a complex desorption mechanism. Initially, electrocatalysts used noble metals to wrap electrodes or arched metal particles on them by electrodeposition. However, the cost of using noble metals is high, and it is also difficult to keep the stability of catalysts in long-term operations [86]. Porous carbonbased materials can greatly compensate for the above-mentioned deficiencies of noble metals. For example, verticaly grown 3D carbon-based materials prepared by CVD or plasma enhanced CVD methods can provide strictly open channels and abundant surface area for the shortest straight path of ions and abundant charge storage sites. This is conducive to improve the rough surface that can further enhance the electrochemical stability. Liang et al. [87] proposed a robust cobalt-nitrogen/carbon catalyst for HER. It can be used in a wide pH range, coupled with suitable anode catalysts, and is suitable for different types of water-splitting devices. During the process of HER, the inactive inorganic cobalt phase was eliminated by acid leaching, and a larger carbon surface was exposed, resulting in an increase in Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area and a raised HER activity. Han et al. [88] prepared a robust HER catalyst by loading ${\mathrm{Ni}}_{2}{\mathrm{P}}$ on 3D few-layer graphene/nickel foam $\left(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{P-G@NF}\right)$ . It shows high performance and activity for electrocatalytic HER when used as the cathode, compared with even Pt. The excellent activity is attributed to the unique 3D interconnected structure,which makes good contact between the conductive materials and the catalyst, graphene layers and highly active ${\mathrm{Ni}}_{2}{\mathrm{P}}$ nanoflakes (Fig. 8(c)).
In addition, active sites are also very crucial for the activity of electrocatalytic HER. By constructing 3D porous structure, increasing the density of active sites can not only improve the performance, but also reduce the catalytic footprint, thereby reducing the cost. McAteer et al. [89] reported that the addition of CNTs on $\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ nanosheets to form 3D porous materials can facilitate electron transport from the current collector to catalytically active sites within the electrode. This porous networktype morphology facilitates the free access of the electrolyte to the inner surface of the electrode, which strongly promotes charge transport to the $\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ active sites and leads to higher HER performance. But not all of these sites will be active as some may have become functionalized during the exfoliation process.
## 3.1.2 HER from methanolysis of sodium borohydride
Metal hydrides are the most important storage reagents for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ carriers. $\mathrm{{NaBH_{4}}}$ is particularly attractive because of its non-volatile, environmentally friendly, and high $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ content. Solvents including, for example, ethanol, methanol, glycerol, and ethylene glycol can be used for the release of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ from $\mathrm{\DeltaNaBH_{4}}$ [90-93]. Among them, methanol has the highest $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ yield, where the release of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ can be expressed as (Eq. (4))
$$
\mathrm{NaBH_{4}+4C H_{3}O H\rightarrow N a B(O C H_{3})_{4}+4H_{2}}
$$
For example, porous carbon-based particles were modified with polyethyleneimine to form amine-functional particles, where the whole reaction was acid catalyzed. The positively surface charged particles significantly promoted the methanol release of catalytic $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ [94]. Zhang et al. [95] further reported that multi-walled carbon nanotubes would significantly facilitate hydrogen transport to the alloy, enabling more hydrogen storage. As shown in Figs. 8(d) and 8(e), this electro-oxidation of additional hydrogen not only enhances the catalytic activity of anode but also improves the utilization of $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ . In this case, the open circuit potential was more negative, which confirms the dominance of $\mathrm{BH}_{4}^{-}$ electrooxidation (Eqs. (5)-(7)) [96]
$$
\mathrm{BH}_{4}^{-}+4\mathrm{OH}^{-}=\mathrm{BO}_{2}^{-}+2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}+4\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{ad}}+4\mathrm{e}^{-}
$$
$$
\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{ad}}+\mathrm{OH}^{-}=\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}+\mathrm{e}^{-}
$$
$$
\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{ad}}+\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{ad}}=\mathrm{H}_{2}
$$
## 3.1.3 HER from methane decomposition
The thermal catalytic decomposition of methane is a heat absorption process; if the temperature exceeds $1{,}200^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ methane can be completely decomposed into hydrogen and carbon [97]. Although transition metal is used to produce a large amount of carbon dioxide by combustion or gasification deposition, the cost is high and the stability is low. Further, the fibrous carbon obtained from metal catalyst usually needs further acid-treatment for purification [98]. Due to their usability, durability, and low cost, carbonaceous catalysts have been used for catalytic methane decomposition (CMD） recently. Methane conversion $(X_{\mathrm{CH_{4}}})$ and hydrogen selectivity $(S_{\mathrm{H}_{2}})$ are defined by (Eqs.(8) and (9)) [98]
$$
X_{\mathrm{CH_{4}}}=\frac{(F_{\mathrm{CH_{4},i n}}-F_{\mathrm{CH_{4},o u t}})}{F_{\mathrm{CH_{4},i n}}}\times100\%
$$
$$
S_{\mathrm{H}_{2}}=\frac{0.5F_{\mathrm{H}_{2},\mathrm{out}}}{\left(F_{\mathrm{CH}_{4},\mathrm{in}}-F_{\mathrm{CH}_{4},\mathrm{out}}\right)}\times100\%
$$
where $X,~S,$ and $F$ represents methane conversion, hydrogen selectivity and gas flow rate (divided into in the column and out the column), respectively.
The trimodal porous carbon-based material prepared by Shen and co-workers [99] has the worst conversion rate of micropores, probably due to the smaller pore size,which prevents effective adsorption of methane on the active sites and dissociating. Mesopores and micropores provide many reactive sites in this hierarchical tripolar pore structure of carbon-based materials, while the pore channels served as transport channels to resist deactivation (Figs. 9(a)-9(c)).
## 3.1.4 HER from pyrolysis-gasification
Through KOH activation with addition of $\mathrm{{Al}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}_{3},$ Zhang et al.[10o]  prepared hierarchical $\mathrm{Ni-La/Al_{2}O_{3}\mathrm{-}C e O_{2}}.$ bamboo charcoal micro-mesoporous carbon catalyst. The catalysts prepared are used for CMD, where $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ reactive selectivity can be maintained at $98\%$ . High-quality hydrogen-rich syngas can be produced from biomass and plastic waste through a two-stage pyrolysis gasification process (Fig.9(d)), in which plastics are considered as the main source for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ and increasing their proportion in the feedstock will raise especially $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ The yields of CO and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in the products are attributed to the carbonylation and decarboxylation reactions of biomass compounds to decarbonize oxygenates. Therefore, the production of CO is proportional to biomass, and the amount of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ produced is inversely proportional to the combustion temperature. At a high temperature of $700^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ $\operatorname{Ni@CNF}$ porous carbon catalyst is beneficial to promote the reformation reaction of ${\mathrm{C}}^{2+}$ components with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to generate $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and CO. In addition, the catalyst is easily gasified by $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at high temperature (Figs. 9(e) and 9(f)） [l0l]. The catalytic methane cracking and reforming reaction can be expressed as following (Eqs. (10)-(16)) [101,102]
$$
\mathrm{CO}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\rightarrow\mathrm{CO}_{2}+\mathrm{H}_{2}
$$
$$
\mathrm{C+H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\rightarrow\mathrm{CO}+\mathrm{H}_{2}
$$
$$
\mathrm{C}+2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\to\mathrm{CH}_{4}+\mathrm{CO}_{2}
$$
$$
\mathrm{CO}+3\mathrm{H}_{2}\to\mathrm{CH}_{4}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}
$$
$$
\mathrm{CO}_{2}+4\mathrm{H}_{2}\to\mathrm{CH}_{4}+2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}
$$
$$
\mathrm{C}_{n}\mathrm{H}_{m}+n\mathrm{CO}_{2}\to(m/2)\mathrm{H}_{2}+2n\mathrm{CO}
$$
$$
\mathrm{CH}_{4}\to\mathbf{C}+2\mathrm{H}_{2}
$$
The relationship between the porous structure and the HER pathway of five main carbon-based materials is summarized in Table l, along with the associated advantages and disadvantages.
## 3.2 Improving strategies
According to the above HER mechanisms, some improving strategies are developed recently, mainly including: (i) single-atom doping, (ii) microwave-assisted carbonization, (ii) spray pyrolysis.
## 3.2.1 Single-atom doping
Semiconductors rely on intrinsic excitation to generate photogenerated carriers. However, exciting insufficient number of carriers will not only yield unsatisfactory conductivity but also make the product easily afected by factors including, reaction temperature, time, and pH [106-108]. Doping of semiconductors, precisely regulating the charge carrier type and concentration in a controllable manner is vital for optoelectronics [109]. Doping single atoms aims to change the lattice, increase dislocations, make ion channels larger, and enhance conductivity. The energy band of the semiconductor will be changed after doping and the doped single-atom sites will play a crucial role in enhancing HER activity [110,11l]. Single-atom catalysts (SACs) could become a new frontier in the field of HER catalysis, due to their great catalytic activity and excellent chemical selectivity [112, l13]. SACs exhibit significantly different catalytic behaviors than metal nanoparticles.Metal-basedatomicallydispersedcatalysts compared with non-metal and/or non-noble catalysts have been more extensively researched, because of their excellent catalytic performance and nearly $100\%$ atom utilization [1l4]. Isolated single metal atoms dispersed on porous carbon-based materials have a high degree of freedom, and can be loaded on the surface, the edge, the framework, and in the aperture. Though isolated atoms are deposited on a substrate, it is easy to tend to migrate and aggregate into clusters or nanoparticles on a substrate. In case of atomical metals escaping from the surfaces of those clusters or nanoparticles, high temperatures are usually required to accelerate the thermal motion of metal atoms and provide suffcient energy to break the metal-metal bonds [ll5]. However, the high surface energy of atomically dispersed metals may easily lead to its agglomeration, and choosing feasible supports is indispensable for their stabilization. Carbon-based supports, such as porous carbon, are prone to strengthening the anchoring of metal atoms due to heteroatoms doping (N, P, O, and S) and defects [116]. Meanwhile, MOFs-derived porous carbon-based materials have uniform and tunable pores, which act like“cages” to encapsulate guest molecules and improve the interaction between host and guest molecules. Most importantly, MOFs have a strong thermal stablity so that during a pyrolysis process they can form strong metal-nitrogen coordination bonds, resulting in an enhancement of the chemical stability of SACs [117]. Li et al. [118] synthesized a
MOFs-808-EDTA (MBT) encapsulated single-atom Pt catalyst (Figs. 10(a) and 10(b)). The $\mathrm{Pt}^{2+}$ ions were facilely captured by postsynthetic modified EDTA ligands anchored at the $Z\mathrm{r}6$ cluster metal nodes. Single-atom Pt-based catalyst shows three orders of magnitude faster in the rate of HER (Fig.lO(c)). This is largely due to the lower hydrogen binding free energy at the single-atom $\mathrm{Pt}$ site anchored at the EDTA ligand of MOFs-808 architecture. However, Pt active centers exhibit high activity in acidic media, to the contrary, catalysts containing Ru species present remarkable HER activity in alkaline media, even exceeding those of advanced HER catalysts under acidic conditions [119]. Besides, according to recent research, N-doped ordered mesoporous carbons are developing as invigorating and novel support, for their rated pore channels, unparalleled confined microenvironment, and abundant N ligands, which make develop more advanced catalysts possible [120]. Shi et al.[121] proposed a novel in-situ synthesis method for preparing Co-N-C catalysts (Figs. 10(d) and 10(e)). As the loading of single-atom of Co increases, more N-doping sites within the carrier need to be used as anchors to stabilize $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}$ atoms, which may affect the delocalized $\pi$ -bonding of the carrier. As shown in Fig.l0(f), as the loading of single-atom of Co over 5.6 $\mathrm{{wt.\%}}$ ， the improvement of photocatalytic activity was not significant. Therefore, there may exist a threshold in single-atom of Co loaded for improving photocatalytic performance.
## 3.2.2 Microwave-assisted carbonization
Microwave-assisted synthesis technology has the advantages of fast heating rate, uniform heating, temperature gradient absent, short reaction time, energy saving, and no pollution [124]. Preparing porous carbon-based material by this method not only greatly reduces the preparation time but also yields porous carbon-based material that has dense and smooth surface, uniform pore distribution, excellent adsorption capacity, improved HER quantum yield, recyclable use, and high stability [l25]. Seza et al. [126] synthesized $\mathrm{g{-}C_{3}N_{4}/S n O_{2}}$ nanocomposites by microwave irradiation. Some urea molecules may be completely thermally decomposed into gaseous products, instead of condensed. Via diffusion or intercalation, these molecules are preferred to be located between the graphitic layers of $\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4}}.$ In the case of intercalated molecules, to some extent, further irradiation of microwave can cause the exothermic decomposition of urea. The resultant pore size is larger and more uniform, and the visible light photocatalytic HER activity is significantly improved (Figs. 1l(a)
and 1l(b)). Sahoo et al. [127] used microwave-assisted method to prepare porous Mo-complex N-rich carbonous sponge as positive electrode, and further heat-treated at a higher temperature to fabricate a more carbonized Mo-complex N-doped sponge as negative electrode. As shown in Fig.ll(c), the two sponges synthesized in a short time were lightweight and porous, and exhibited excellent specific capacitance.
## 3.2.3 Spray pyrolysis
Spray pyrolysis refers to spray a solution containing metal salt into a high temperature in the form of a mist. The solvent evaporates immediately, and metal salt thermally decomposes. Then solid phase precipitates due to supersaturation, thereby directly obtaining nano-powders [128]. The particle size or the thickness of the film formed depends on the equipment and operating conditions. The advantage of this method is mainly the short reaction time, which is very suitable for element doping [129]. Ye et al. [130] proposed a simple ultrasonic spray pyrolysis method to fabricate amorphous MoSx/CNTs hybrid nanospheres (Fig. 12(a)). The resultant catalysts are highly durable and capable of mas production. MoS,/CNTs catalyst prepared at $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ exhibits low overpotential with a Tafel slope of $36~\mathrm{mV\cdotdec^{-1}}$ (Figs. 12(b) and 12(c)). Through spray pyrolysis, Kim et al. [13l] uniformly decorated CoP nanoparticles on conductive CNTs framework microspheres to form macroporous framework, facilitating electrolyte penetration and maximizing active sites exposure. The CoP-CNTs catalyst not only enhances the electron transfer along the microspheres but also minimizes the polarization of the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ gas during HER. In addition, it can maintain an excellent HER activity after more than 2,000 cycles (Figs. 12(d)-12(f)).
These above three modification methods have their own advantages and disadvantages. Table 2 summarizes the strategies, advantages, and disadvantages of modification of porous carbonbased materials as HER catalyst, aiming to optimize the catalytic performance of porous carbon-based materials and improve the HER efficiency.
## 4 Conclusions and perspectives
Being a new type of porous crystalline material, 3D porous carbonbased material has a high specific surface area, ordered and interconnected network structure, and adjustable porosity. Because it is low-cost and environmentally friendly, this kind of material is often used as a precursor, and widely applied in the field of catalysis, sensors, energy storage, and energy conversion. As highlighted in this review, each type of porous carbon-based material has its own advantages. Porous CNTs exhibit excellent heat transfer properties with rather large aspect ratios, which can be further enhanced by metal doping. As a star material for photocatalysis, $\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4}}$ is widely used in this kind of reaction. However, it is impeded by its bulk structure and small specific surface area. Via introducing pore structure into $\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4},}$ their photocatalytic activity could be further improved. On one hand,
COFs- and MOFs-derived porous carbon-based materials have a large number of pore structures, but controlling their size and profile for different catalytic applications is not straightforward. On the other hand, biomass-derived porous carbon-based materials are abundant, environmentally friendly, and have unique advantages in sewage treatment and gas adsorption.
In this review, we also systematically summarize four main methods for HER from 3D porous carbon-based materials. Firstly, although HER through hydrolysis produces no by-products, the efficiency is relatively low, and both the stability and toxicity of catalysts needed to be improved. Secondly, sodium hydrogenation can not only produce and supply hydrogen instantly but also provide high-purity hydrogen energy for portable devices. Compared with optoelectronic catalysis, the response conditions are facile. In particular, it can be carried out at low temperature without providing additional energy. Thirdly, the heatdecomposition of methane to HER is that during methane pyrolysis, methane is heated to high temperatures that the molecule itself becomes unstable,which in turn decomposes into hydrogen. Mainly transition metals such as Fe, Co, and $\mathrm{Ni},$ as well as activated carbon are chosen as supported catalysts. Finally, through pyrolysis-gasification, HER can not only take full use of biomass resources, but also alleviate fossil energy consumption, promoting economic growth. However, the associated efficiency is still low.
Although 3D porous carbon-based materials have made great progress in both approaches of synthesis and application, they have not been studied in sufficiently detail for improving the performance of HER. Rational design of efficient hydrogen HER based-on 3D porous carbon-based materials is desirable in the near future. The probable design perspectives could be summarized as following:
(a) When porous carbon-based materials are used in HER, how to take full use of the pore-like structure is of great importance. In addition, to expose more active sites, this pore-like structure can also work as template with holes to construct a special optical micro-cavity resonant grating structure to enhance sunlight absorption. This design can not only improve the reactivity but also facilitate convenient catalyst recovery.
(b) At present, the yield of HER through electrolysis is less than $1\%$ . Among the available methods, the proportion of HER by proton exchange membrane is significantly higher than that by other electrolysis methods. The preparation of composite membranes based on porous carbon-based materials can further exert the thermal stability, chemical stability, and high mechanical strength of porous carbon-based materials, thereby promoting further the improvement of industrialization.
(c) The progress of direct cracking of methane for HER is cleanly without producing any CO or $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . Formerly, Fe, Co, and Ni have been used to improve methane conversion when doping in semiconductor. However, the stability may not be maintained at high temperature. More importantly, a multi-functional catalyst using porous carbon-based material as substrate and doped with different metal atoms or particle without adding any additives can achieve a higher activity at room temperature and reduce toxic byproducts.
(d) The structure of pores and their position profile of porous carbon-based materials are usually difficult to control. Compared with current main methods, the regulation of lattice structure does not require additional organic solvents, which is very suitable for carbon-based materials, especially structurally symmetric carbonbased materials, such as graphene. The aim of this favorable strategy is to enhance the electron transport rate and electrical conductivity of 2D carbon-based materials in the fields of sensing and energy storage.
(e) In HER, the distribution of active sites (on or in the plane of the carbon-based materials) is mainly responsible for improving the activity. However, the difference between the interfacial active sites and the internal active sites has not been deeply explored. In addition, the connections between different components (substrates, doped atoms, heterojunctions, and lattice defects) and active sites have not been clearly defined, neither is the synergistic reaction of different components with each other during the reaction. The reaction mechanisms need to be clarified in the future research, which is helpful for a rational design of efficient HER porous carbon-based catalysts.
(f) Liquid ammonia, methanol, and hydride for hydrogen storage have mature products and wide applications in foreign countries but they are still in the experimental stage in China. Porous carbon-based materials, including carbon paper and carbon cloth, especially carbon fiber, have the advantages of low resistance, high permeability, and high mechanical strength. Unfortunately, there is still a big gap between China and developed countries in high-performance carbon fiber materials. Porous and multifunctional biomass carbon fiber materials have made great contributions in the field of HER and storage. The technology for developing porous and multifunctional biomass carbon fiber materials is expected to accelerate the development.
